        <p class="profile-detail-region">Culture · Americas</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Zapotec civilisation</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Homeland</span><span class="info-stat-val">The Central Valleys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oaxaca">Oaxaca</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> (Etla, Ocotlán and Mitla), about 200 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico-city">Mexico City</a>; key sites Monte Albán, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a>, Mitla, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lambityeco">Lambityeco</a>, Dainzú, Yagul, El Palmillo, Zaachila and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guiengola">Guiengola</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-tehuantepec">Isthmus of Tehuantepec</a></span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">Rosario phase (700–500 BCE); Monte Alban 1 (late phase 400–100 BCE); Monte Alban 2 (c. 100 BCE – 200 CE), the peak of expansion; Monte Albán the largest city of the southern Mexican highlands until about 700 CE; Aztec conquest of the area in 1458; Spanish rule from 1521</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">Monte Albán and its monumental buildings, ball courts and tombs; one of the first writing systems of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>; a calendar; building J, the &quot;Conquest Slab&quot;; finely worked gold jewellery; the ruins of Mitla; canal irrigation</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">How we know them</span><span class="info-stat-val">Excavations at Monte Albán, begun by Alfonso Caso in the 1930s; the work of Joyce Marcus and Kent V. Flannery; colonial-era sources on the gods, compiled by Michael Lind; legends written down after the Spanish conquest; finds by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>'s National Institute of Anthropology and History (INAH)</span></div>
