    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Monte Albán was founded on a mountain in the no-man's-land between three rival valley societies, and its early pottery suggests settlers came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-jose-mogote">San José Mogote</a>.</li><li>The Zapotec developed a logosyllabic writing system thought to be one of the first in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>; it remains undeciphered.</li><li>The Zapotec called themselves by a variant of Be'ena'a, the Cloud People, from their elite's belief that they descended from beings who lived among the clouds.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Building J at Monte Albán, shaped like an arrowhead, carries more than 40 carved stones that archaeologists read as the provinces the Zapotec controlled; upside-down heads on them are believed to stand for rulers killed in conquest.</p></aside>
    </section>

